Title: Casebook chapter about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# Foundations of Likelihood of Confusion: Section 2(d), TTAB Practice, and Appellate Review

This chapter sets out the doctrinal foundation and practical architecture governing refusals under Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act, the procedural posture and evidentiary dynamics of Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) oppositions, and the appellate review rules that shape what issues survive to the Federal Circuit. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., 2025 WL ___ (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025), supplies a recent and instructive example: the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that the parties’ services were highly similar (the second DuPont factor), but vacated and remanded the Board’s treatment of third‑party use (the sixth DuPont factor) and the marks’ overall commercial impression (the first DuPont factor) because the Board applied inconsistent scopes of similarity across factors. That outcome illustrates three persistent themes: (1) the DuPont framework is flexible but must be applied consistently; (2) the TTAB’s findings rest on the evidentiary record made below; and (3) appellate courts review legal questions de novo but defer to the Board’s factual findings when supported by substantial evidence.

## Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act: text, purpose, and where refusals fit

Section 2(d) provides that the Patent and Trademark Office shall refuse registration if a mark “so resembles” a previously registered mark “as to be likely, when used on or in connection with the goods or services of the applicant, to cause confusion, or to cause mistake, or to deceive.” 15 U.S.C. § 1052(d). The statutory scheme balances two policy goals: protecting consumers from confusion about source and protecting owners’ goodwill by granting a right to exclude confusingly similar marks in the registration context. A Section 2(d) refusal is a refusal of registration—it does not by itself adjudicate infringement in the marketplace—but registration is valuable (constructive notice, prima facie evidence of validity and ownership), so disputes over registration often determine whether new entrants can obtain those benefits.

Oppositions and cancellation proceedings before the TTAB are the routine vehicles for resolving Section 2(d) claims in the administrative process. The Board applies a multi‑factor framework to determine whether confusion is likely; registration will be refused if the Board finds a likelihood of confusion under that framework.

## TTAB practice: from filing to opposition to decision

The administrative path begins with an application filed in the USPTO. Many applicants file intent‑to‑use (ITU) applications under Section 1(b); the mark is published for opposition before use is required. During prosecution, third parties may file letters of protest, which can bring evidence to the examiner’s attention but do not substitute for a formal opposition. If the mark survives examination, it publishes in the Official Gazette, triggering a statutory 30‑day window (extendable) for any party to oppose. An opposition is litigated before the TTAB under Board rules: pleadings, discovery, testimony periods with written direct and rebuttal evidence (declarations and exhibits), and an oral hearing.

Practical implications visible in Apex: Apex filed ITU applications for ASPIRE BANK; CC Serve submitted a letter of protest in prosecution, but the examiner nevertheless published the mark. CC Serve then filed an opposition asserting Section 2(d). The Board heard evidence and made findings about the parties’ services and third‑party uses from materials in the record. Those materials—what was introduced into evidence—proved decisive on appeal. That procedural chain highlights two rules every practitioner must respect: (1) the letter of protest can alert the examiner but cannot substitute for a fully litigated opposition; and (2) evidence not put into the TTAB record will generally not be entertained on appeal.

## The DuPont framework: flexibility, relevant factors, and the Board’s obligations

The Board assesses likelihood of confusion using the factors laid out in In re E. I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357 (C.C.P.A. 1973) (the “DuPont factors”). There are thirteen listed factors, including: similarity of the marks (factor one); similarity of the goods/services and channels of trade (factor two); conditions of purchase and consumer sophistication (factor four); evidence of actual confusion (factor six is number and nature of similar marks? — note that courts and the Board sometimes enumerate differently; the controlling principle is that the Board should consider relevant DuPont factors). The DuPont test is not a mathematical checklist; the Board need not discuss every factor, only those that are relevant and supported by the record. M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378, 1382 (Fed. Cir. 2006).

Two points about DuPont application recur in appellate opinions and in practice:

- The factors are flexible and contextual. Some factors may be dispositive in the right case (e.g., identical marks for identical goods), while others may be neutral. The Board’s task is to analyze the factors supported by evidence and to explain its reasoning.

- Consistency in scope matters. When the Board defines the scope of relevant goods, channels of trade, and consumers in analyzing one factor, it must reasonably retain that scope (or explain why it changes) when analyzing related factors. Apex demonstrates why: the Board found Apex’s “banking and financing services” and CC Serve’s “credit card services” highly similar (factor two). But in assessing third‑party use (factor six)—which tests the mark’s practical strength by surveying similar marks—the Board limited the pool of third‑party Aspire marks to those expressly used for “credit card services,” excluding other banking or financial uses. The Federal Circuit found that inconsistent. If the services are “highly similar,” then third‑party uses in the same broader field (banking/finance) are probative of weakness; restricting consideration to one subset without justification risks error.

## Identifications of goods and services: the written description controls, and default presumptions

The Board compares the parties’ legally defined goods and services as they appear in the application or registration. The test is not whether, in a particular market, the parties actually compete, but whether the goods/services as identified could be related in commerce. See, e.g., Coach Servs. Inc. v. Triumph Learning LLC, 668 F.3d 1356, 1369 (Fed. Cir. 2012). Where an identification is broad (e.g., “banking and financing services”), the Board often interprets that scope through dictionary definitions, the Trademark ID Manual, evidence of industry practice, or third‑party registrations that show how the descriptive terms are commonly used. The Board also applies the presumption that the channels of trade and classes of consumers identified in a registration are those contemplated by the identifications; absent limiting language, the Board assumes that the registrant will use the mark on the full sweep of the identified services and in all normal channels of trade for them. This is why meticulous drafting of identifications matters.

In Apex, the Board construed “credit card services” broadly—beyond merely issuing a physical card to include account servicing, processing, authorization, monitoring, and counseling. The Board then concluded that dictionary and regulatory definitions of “banking” and “finance” encompass issuance of credit (including through credit cards), and therefore the services overlapped and were, in part, legally identical. That construction of the identifications controlled the second‑factor analysis and should have controlled related factor analyses absent a reasoned departure.

## Third‑party use and the sixth DuPont factor: crowding, commercial weakness, and proper scope

Third‑party registrations and marketplace uses of similar marks are relevant to show that a mark is conceptually or commercially weak—that is, entitled to a narrower scope of protection. See Palm Bay Imps., Inc. v. Veuve Clicquot Ponsardin Maison Fondee En 1772, 396 F.3d 1369, 1373 (Fed. Cir. 2005); Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters. LLC, 794 F.3d 1334, 1338–39 (Fed. Cir. 2015). The Board may rely on samples of third‑party registrations, advertisements, and other evidence of use to support a finding that a mark is not strong. But the probative set of third‑party uses is those that are similar in mark and used on goods or services that are similar to those at issue. Similarity here does not require identity. See Olde Tyme Foods, Inc. v. Roundy’s, Inc., 961 F.2d 200, 204 (Fed. Cir. 1992).

Apex’s appeal turned on whether the Board properly defined the universe of third‑party uses to consider. Apex introduced a broad array of third‑party uses of “Aspire” on banking and finance services (42 marks), while the Board limited consideration to nine marks used explicitly for credit card services, because it viewed the relevant consumer as the credit card user. The Federal Circuit held that this narrowing conflicted with the Board’s own finding—made in the second‑factor analysis—that the parties’ services were highly similar, even partially legally identical. Once the Board has found such similarity, third‑party uses across the broader class of banking/finance services are relevant to show weakness. The lesson: when arguing weakness through third‑party use, show (with evidence) how those third‑party goods/services relate to your opponent’s identifications and to the identifications at issue. Conversely, when the Board finds relatedness, it should not arbitrarily narrow the scope of third‑party evidence it will consider.

## The first DuPont factor and commercial impression: why strength and weakness interact

The first DuPont factor—the similarity of the marks in their entirety as to appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression—asks whether, viewed as a whole in marketplace context, the marks are likely to be confused. Commercial impression is not static; it is informed by the mark’s conceptual distinctiveness and by its commercial strength in the marketplace. If a mark is shown to be weak because many third parties use the same or similar terms for related goods, the scope of protection narrows and the first‑factor analysis may tilt differently.

Apex illustrates this interaction. The Board assessed the parties’ marks and concluded they were similar enough to weigh in favor of CC Serve on the first factor. But because the court vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis for improperly narrowing the universe of third‑party uses, it also remanded the first factor: reassessment of third‑party evidence could change the mark’s commercial strength and thus the marks’ overall commercial impressions. That is a routine pattern: when an error in assessing evidence of weakness is corrected, the entirety‑of‑mark analysis frequently must be revisited because commercial impression is interdependent with evidence of marketplace crowding.

## Standards of review on appeal: de novo law, substantial evidence fact

Two interconnected review rules govern appeals from the TTAB:

- Questions of law—primarily the legal conclusion whether a likelihood of confusion exists—are reviewed de novo. Likelihood of confusion is a legal question informed by subsidiary factual findings. See M2 Software, 450 F.3d at 1382.

- Factual findings, including determinations under individual DuPont factors (e.g., the factual scope of the parties’ services, credibility of witnesses, and whether third parties have used similar marks), are reviewed for substantial evidence. Substantial evidence is “such relevant evidence as a reasonable mind might accept as adequate to support a conclusion.” Consol. Edison Co. of N.Y. v. NLRB, 305 U.S. 197, 229 (1938); Bose Corp. v. QSC Audio Prods., Inc., 293 F.3d 1367, 1370 (Fed. Cir. 2002).

Why the distinction matters: appellate courts will overturn a Board’s legal conclusions when the Board applied the wrong legal standard or failed to make the required findings; but they will sustain factual conclusions if reasonable evidence supports them, even if the appellate court might have reached a different factual conclusion. In Apex, the Federal Circuit found the Board’s factual finding that the services were highly similar to be supported by substantial evidence and therefore affirmed the Board on the second DuPont factor. But the court exercised de novo review of whether the Board’s legal approach to the sixth DuPont factor was correct in light of its earlier factual finding, and found error—thus vacating and remanding.

## The record rule: build the case before the TTAB

A central administrative‑law limitation is the record rule: the Federal Circuit reviews the administrative record compiled before the Board; new evidence cannot be introduced on appeal. M2 Software, 450 F.3d at 1382; 37 C.F.R. § 2.142(d) (motion practice for reopening record before the Board in limited circumstances). Thus litigants must assemble their proof at the TTAB: declarations, third‑party registrations and samples of use, advertising and sales materials, consumer surveys, expert testimony, and any pertinent dictionary or regulatory definitions.

Apex underscores this imperative. Both parties presented evidence—definitions, third‑party examples, prosecution history, and a description of how CC Serve’s business model uses bank partners to issue ASPIRE‑branded cards—and the court’s review turned on what the Board had actually considered. On remand, the Board will act on the same record (plus any additional evidence properly admitted during remand proceedings), not on new materials offered first to the Federal Circuit.

Practical consequences: identify early what you must prove at the TTAB and collect that evidence. If you intend to show relatedness across product lines, put in third‑party registrations and use examples demonstrating the interrelatedness. If you intend to show weakness, include representative third‑party uses across the whole field. If you intend to show consumer sophistication or limits on channels of trade, present evidence of how the goods/services are marketed and sold.

## Anchoring precedents: DuPont, M2 Software, Bose, and modern refinements

Several precedents frame modern Section 2(d) practice:

- In re E. I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357 (C.C.P.A. 1973): established the multi‑factor likelihood‑of‑confusion test and the principle that courts need only address relevant DuPont factors.

- M2 Software, Inc. v. M2 Commc’ns, Inc., 450 F.3d 1378 (Fed. Cir. 2006): clarified standards of review for Board decisions and reiterated the record rule.

- Bose Corp. v. QSC Audio Prods., Inc., 293 F.3d 1367 (Fed. Cir. 2002): reaffirmed that DuPont factors are findings of fact reviewable for substantial evidence.

- Palm Bay Imps., Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin: each addresses third‑party use and how the “crowded field” argument affects a mark’s scope. Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin show that the quantity and quality of third‑party uses matter; a handful of similar third‑party marks may be insufficient to show weakness, whereas voluminous and probative examples may.

- Olde Tyme Foods and cases like Coach Services: reinforce that similarity of goods/services does not require identity—relatedness for likelihood‑of‑confusion purposes can be established by evidence showing that the goods/services are complementary, marketed in the same channels, or otherwise connected in consumers’ minds.

Apex fits within this lineage. The Federal Circuit relied on DuPont and the standards articulated in these cases to review whether the Board applied the right legal approach and relied on substantial evidence for its factual findings.

## Applying these principles: what Apex teaches practitioners

Apex offers several concrete takeaways:

- Define and prove relatedness. To prevail on relatedness (factor two), demonstrate with evidence—dictionary definitions, third‑party registrations, industry materials, or business practice descriptions—how the identified goods/services overlap in function, channels, or commercial purpose. In Apex the Board’s careful parsing of the identifications and the dictionary/regulatory definitions supported its finding that banking/finance and credit card services are highly similar.

- Be comprehensive with third‑party evidence. If you will argue that a mark is weak, submit a representative cross‑section of third‑party registrations and actual uses across the related field. Don’t assume the Board will limit its inquiry to a narrow slice—if the services are related, third‑party uses across the broader field are relevant. Apex required remand because the Board selectively considered third‑party uses inconsistent with its own finding of relatedness.

- Preserve the record. Because appeals lie to the Federal Circuit under substantial‑evidence review, evidence not introduced below is generally lost. Use TTAB discovery rules, and plan early for the kinds of proof the Board treats as dispositive: third‑party use, consumer sophistication, channels of trade, and actual confusion evidence (if available).

- Think about scope consistency. Once the Board defines the relevant consumer and channels of trade for one factor, it should justify any departure when treating another factor. The Federal Circuit will scrutinize inconsistencies that change the probative universe—especially when the Board’s own factual findings make a different universe reasonable.

- Anticipate interplay among factors. Commercial strength and commercial impression (factor one) depend in part on evidence adduced under the sixth factor. When you develop your case, present both conceptual and commercial evidence of strength or weakness so the Board can evaluate the marks’ entirety in context.

## Remedies and appellate posture: affirmance-in-part, vacatur-in-part, remand

When the Federal Circuit finds that some Board findings are supported by substantial evidence and other parts suffer from legal error or lack support, it commonly issues an affirmance‑in‑part, vacatur‑in‑part, and remand. That is what happened in Apex: the court affirmed the finding that the services were highly similar, vacated the Board’s constrained treatment of third‑party uses, and remanded factors one and six for reconsideration under a coherent scope. Remand is a common remedial posture when further factfinding or reanalysis by the Board is needed to apply the correct legal standard or to integrate a corrected evidentiary scope into the remaining factors.

On remand, the Board may: (1) reconsider the sixth DuPont factor using the broader set of third‑party uses; (2) revisit the first DuPont factor in light of any altered view of the mark’s commercial strength; and (3) make any consequential adjustments to the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion conclusion. Parties can seek to introduce additional evidence during remand proceedings only under the Board’s procedural rules; usually, remand operates on the existing record unless the Board exercises its discretion to reopen for limited purposes.

## Practical checklist for TTAB practitioners (derived from Apex)

- Draft identifications carefully. Remember that the comparison is based on the identifications in the application or registration.

- Anticipate relevant channels of trade and consumers, and put evidence into the record that demonstrates who the consumers are and how the goods/services are marketed.

- Assemble robust third‑party evidence if you will argue weakness: registrations plus samples of actual marketplace use across all related goods/services.

- If you challenge relatedness, put in documentary evidence (industry materials, contracts showing partnerships, testimonial evidence) to show that services are not just theoretically related but are marketed or provided in complementary ways.

- Use dictionary definitions and the Trademark ID Manual to support construction of terms, but also be ready to supplement with real‑world evidence showing how terms are used.

- Preserve every piece of relevant evidence at the TTAB; do not expect to add new proof on appeal.

- If the Board reaches an adverse legal conclusion, consider whether any factual findings underlying that conclusion lack substantial‑evidence support or whether the Board applied an inconsistent legal standard across factors—those are the typical grounds for vacatur and remand.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. reinforces familiar but important lessons. The DuPont framework demands fact‑driven, evidence‑based analysis; the Board’s findings are entitled to deference when supported by substantial evidence; and legal errors or inconsistent scope applications will lead to vacatur and remand. For practitioners, the lesson is clear: build a complete, coherent record at the TTAB and make the Board’s job straightforward by supplying the evidence and reasoned arguments needed to support each DuPont factor you rely on.

# Relatedness of Services (DuPont Factor 2) in the Financial Sector

The second DuPont factor asks whether the goods or services identified in the parties’ registrations or applications are related in such a way that consumers are likely to believe they come from the same source. In the financial sector—where banks, card issuers, program managers, servicers, and other intermediaries share channels, customers, and marks—this inquiry is frequently decisive. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. (Fed. Cir. 2025) provides a compact illustration of the principles courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (Board) apply: tribunals rely on the words of the identifications, will find relatedness even where entities play different roles in a program (issuer v. servicer), and treat legacy or deleted identification terms by reference to ordinary meaning and dictionary sources. This chapter explains the legal test, shows how to read identifications, unpacks “legally identical, in part,” and offers practice guidance for branding and litigation in the banking and credit‑card space.

## The legal test: relatedness does not require identity or direct competition

The governing principle is simple but broad: the services need not be identical or directly competitive to be related. The Board and courts ask whether “the respective products are related in some manner and/or [that] the circumstances surrounding their marketing are such that they could give rise to the mistaken belief that they emanate from the same source.” Coach Servs. Inc. v. Triumph Learning LLC, 668 F.3d 1356, 1369 (Fed. Cir. 2012). Thus, the inquiry is both (1) a product/service‑to‑product/service comparison and (2) an assessment of the marketplace context and channels of trade. Evidence relevant to this factor includes: the identifications themselves, dictionary definitions, evidence of overlap in channels of trade and consumers, testimony or publications showing that services are commonly offered by the same companies, and third‑party registrations demonstrating that different services are often provided under a single mark.

In Apex, the Board concluded—and the Federal Circuit affirmed—that credit card services (CC Serve) and banking/financing services (Apex) are “highly similar.” The Board relied on the plain meanings of the identifications and on third‑party registrations indicating that banking and credit card services commonly emanate from a single source. The upshot: where the descriptive sweep of one party’s identification encompasses activities that are a routine part of another party’s offering (e.g., extending credit through cards), DuPont factor two will often favor finding relatedness.

## Reading identifications: words control; marketplace reality is secondary

A foundational rule in trademark examination and oppositions is that the scope of protection depends primarily on the express words of the application/registration identification. That means tribunals first determine what the listing covers, based on its plain language, not on the actual business model or the registrant’s particular way of conducting commerce. If the identification literally encompasses overlapping services, the services may be treated as related for DuPont factor two even if, in practice, one party never performs part of the covered activity.

Two consequences follow:

- When drafting identifications applicants should remember that broad, generic wording (for example, “banking and financing services”) will be read in its ordinary sense and can sweep in activities that create overlap with other marks. A broad identification therefore increases vulnerability to opposition from owners of related marks.
- Conversely, an applicant facing an opposition can sometimes limit the scope of its identification (by amendment, restriction, or affidavit of use showing narrow channels) to avoid overlap. But absent such narrowing, the Board and courts are bound to the plain wording.

Apex shows the doctrine in action. Apex filed to register ASPIRE BANK for “banking and financing services.” CC Serve’s registration recited “credit card services.” The Board examined the plain meanings of those terms and concluded they overlap: dictionaries show that “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” can include extending credit through cards; thus the identifications overlap irrespective of how Apex intended to operate (internet bank) or how CC Serve structures its business (partnering with banks).

## Defining legacy terms: interpreting “credit card services” after removal from the ID Manual

Trademark Office practice and the Board often cite the Trademark ID Manual for standard identifications; when a term has been removed from the ID Manual, tribunals fall back on ordinary meaning, dictionary definitions, and record evidence to ascertain scope. Apex illustrates that approach. The ID Manual entry for “credit card services” had been deleted after CC Serve’s registration, so the Board turned to dictionaries and to an analysis of the usual functions associated with credit cards: issuing cards, processing transactions and authorizations, account management, monitoring/alerts, and counseling on credit.

Practitioners should therefore expect that:

- When an ID Manual entry is absent or has been revised, tribunals will construe the term according to its plain and ordinary meaning, possibly supplemented by dictionary definitions, industry sources, and the parties’ evidentiary submissions.
- Evidence showing common consumer understanding or the typical bundle of services associated with a labeled offering (e.g., what “credit card services” normally include) can be decisive in showing overlap.

In Apex the Board’s dictionary-based construction supported a finding of partial legal identity between the parties’ services, because the ordinary meaning of banking and financing includes providing credit and related account services.

## “Legally identical, in part”: what partial identity means and why it matters

Courts use the term “legally identical” to describe situations where the identifications are the same for at least some goods/services. “Legally identical, in part” means that one or more services in one identification literally include services identified in the other party’s application/registration. This partial identity is significant because a finding of legal identity on any subset of the listed services often weighs heavily in the likelihood-of-confusion analysis.

Why partial identity matters:

- If a claim covers identical goods/services (even only in part), there is no need to probe marketplace realities for those identical services—the overlap is established by the identifications themselves.
- Partial identity can “tip” factor two strongly toward a finding of relatedness because it eliminates the need to show channels, customers, or typical marketing—identical scope in the identification is dispositive for that overlapping slice.

In Apex the Board found that, to the extent “credit card services” are encompassed by the ordinary meaning of “banking and financing,” the services are legally identical in part. That conclusion supported a finding that factor two weighed heavily in favor of confusion because the overlap was not merely speculative; it flowed from the identifications’ plain meanings.

## Third‑party registrations as evidence that banking, financing, and credit‑card services emanate from a single source

Third‑party registrations are admissible evidence that certain goods or services are of a kind that may emanate from a single source under one mark. While these registrations do not establish marketplace use of the marks, they are probative of how the PTO has allowed goods/services to be combined under single registrations—useful circumstantial evidence that consumers encounter such combinations.

Key points about third‑party registrations:

- They are most persuasive when the registrations show a pattern: many registrations combining credit card services with banking or financing services suggest that the relevant trade commonly uses a single mark across those services.
- They do not, on their own, prove consumer perception or actual marketplace co‑branding, but they strengthen the inference that consumers expect various financial services to come from the same source.
- Their probative value depends on their relevance to the services at issue. If the Board has already concluded the services are highly similar, the universe of relevant third‑party registrations should be at least as broad as the overlap found.

Apex demonstrates both the power and the limits of this evidence. The Board relied on third‑party registrations showing combined financial and card services to support relatedness under factor two. On appeal, the Federal Circuit affirmed factor two but vacated the Board’s narrower handling of third‑party marks under DuPont factor six—holding that once the Board finds high similarity, it must consider all relevant third‑party uses and registrations of marks used on similar services, not a truncated subset. That interplay between factors 2 and 6 is important for litigators: evidence marshalled to show relatedness can also bear on commercial strength and market crowdedness.

## Coach Services and related authority: overlap without direct competition

Apex’s result aligns squarely with Coach Services v. Triumph Learning, where the Federal Circuit confirmed that relatedness may be found when goods or services are marketed in a way that could create a source‑association, regardless of direct competition. Other precedents—Olde Tyme Foods (relatedness without identity), Juice Generation (crowded field effect), and Palm Bay (weakness via third‑party use)—illustrate the tools courts use. Collectively these cases show:

- Courts do not require the parties to sell identical items in the same store or to be direct competitors to find relatedness.
- The existence of common marketing channels, overlapping consumers, or routine co‑branding by others in the industry suffices.
- The Board and courts give weight to industry practice: if banks commonly put their marks on both deposit accounts and card products, consumers are likely to associate marks across those services.

Thus a servicer that never issues cards can still be related to an issuing bank for DuPont factor two if the marketing and consumer interaction create linkage.

## Program managers and bank partnerships: why role distinctions seldom defeat relatedness

Modern credit card programs often involve distinct players: a sponsoring/issuing bank, a program manager or servicer, and a brand or marketing partner. Trademark disputes sometimes rest on whether a non‑issuing party’s use should be treated as separate for likelihood of confusion purposes. Apex instructs that such structural distinctions do not automatically negate relatedness.

Why role distinctions are often irrelevant for factor two:

- Consumers may never know or care which entity performs back‑office issuance or servicing; they relate the customer experience (statements, rewards, customer service) to the same brand.
- Service descriptions in identifications are controlling. If an applicant’s identification covers “banking and financing services” and an opponent’s registration covers “credit card services,” the legal overlap exists regardless of an entity’s internal role division.
- Evidence that the public encounters a unified brand across issuing and servicing (co‑branded cards, card materials, marketing) strengthens the conclusion that consumers could believe services emanate from the same source.

In Apex CC Serve did not itself issue cards; it partners with banks that issue ASPIRE‑branded cards while CC Serve services accounts. That model did not defeat the Board’s finding of relatedness because the ordinary consumer’s perception of card services and bank services can cohere under one mark.

## Practice pointers: drafting and defending identifications in financial branding

For counsel advising banks, fintechs, or servicers, strategic identification drafting and evidentiary planning can materially affect DuPont factor two outcomes. The following practical guidance flows from the law illustrated by Apex.

For applicants seeking to maximize protection:
- Consider broad identifications if broad protection is desired, but recognize that broad wording invites opposition from owners of related marks.
- File for multiple, tiered identifications (e.g., separate classes or narrower wording) when possible to hedge risk.

For applicants seeking to minimize conflict risk:
- Narrow the identification to the specific services actually provided (for example, “online personal deposit accounts” rather than “banking services”) and be prepared to submit clarifying declarations or specimen evidence.
- If a mark will be used only in conjunction with a partner bank (e.g., as a white‑label servicer), consider disclaimers or statements of limitation and make the record clear as to the narrow scope of use.

For defendants/opponents:
- Emphasize the plain meaning of the opponent’s identification and use dictionaries and industry publications to show overlap.
- Produce third‑party registrations and evidence of co‑branding to show that consumers commonly encounter the services under a single mark.
- Show how marketing channels and consumers overlap (e.g., shared online portals, joint statements, co‑branded materials).

For litigation and appeals:
- When the PTO/Board construes an identification too narrowly or inconsistently across DuPont factors, preserve the issue: courts will scrutinize inconsistencies (Apex vacated the Board’s factor‑six analysis because it imposed a narrower scope than adopted under factor two).
- Gather marketplace evidence (specimens, screenshots, marketing agreements) to show how the public encounters the services and brands.
- Use surveys selectively; courts accept well‑designed consumer surveys showing source association, but the absence of a survey is not fatal where identifications overlap.

Sample drafting alternatives (to reduce overlap risk):
- Instead of “banking and financing services,” consider “online deposit account services; accepting deposits in connection with online banking” or “consumer checking and savings accounts; automated teller machine services.”
- For card program managers: “credit card program administration and servicing for issuers” (if accurate), rather than a generic “credit card services” identification that could be read to mean issuance.

## Litigation angle: what to raise on appeal

When the Board’s finding on factor two rests on the identification and is supported by record evidence, the Federal Circuit applies the substantial‑evidence standard to fact findings and reviews legal conclusions de novo. Appellate success typically requires showing either that the Board misread the identifications or that the factual record cannot support the determination of relatedness. Apex demonstrates that a solid factual and documentary record—plain‑meaning definitions, third‑party registrations, and examples of co‑branding—will survive appellate scrutiny. Conversely, where the Board’s analysis of relatedness is inconsistent with its treatment of other DuPont factors (as the Federal Circuit identified with factor six here), appellate courts will demand coherence and may vacate and remand for reconsideration.

## Conclusion

In the financial services context, DuPont factor two resolves frequently against applicants with broad identifications because customers encounter a wide range of related services under common brands. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates the controlling procedures: start with the identification’s plain meaning (using dictionaries and the ID Manual when appropriate), treat partial legal identity seriously, and use third‑party registrations and marketplace evidence to show that banking, financing, and credit‑card services commonly emanate from a single source. Practitioners should draft identifications with care, gather and present market evidence that clarifies how consumers experience services, and be prepared to challenge inconsistent DuPont treatment on appeal.

# Commercial Strength, Crowded Fields, and Third-Party Use (DuPont Factor 6)

The sixth DuPont factor—“the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods”—is the primary vehicle for proving that a senior mark should receive a narrowed scope of protection because the relevant marketplace is “crowded.” Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., decided by the Federal Circuit in 2025, illustrates both the substantive role of that factor and a procedural point of immediate practical importance: the tribunal must define the relevant goods or services consistently across DuPont factors. This chapter explains how courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) treat the interplay between conceptual distinctiveness and commercial strength, how third‑party evidence is used to measure market crowding, what counts as “similar” goods or services for factor six, and how litigants should build and present a persuasive factor‑six record.

DuPont and the crowded‑field intuition. Under the DuPont framework, factor six asks whether there are numerous similar marks used on similar goods or services. The theory is simple: when many parties use similar marks in a sector, consumers become accustomed to distinguishing among them; the senior mark is therefore less commercially strong and entitled to a narrower scope of protection. The Federal Circuit has repeatedly applied that intuition: voluminous third‑party use supports narrowing (Juice Generation, Inc. v. GS Enters. LLC), while only a handful of examples may be insufficient (Jack Wolfskin Ausrustung Fur Draussen GmbH & Co. KGAA v. New Millennium Sports, S.L.U.). But those decisions do not announce a numeric threshold; the inquiry is qualitative and contextual.

Conceptual versus commercial strength

Two different ideas are important when courts talk about “strength”: conceptual strength and commercial strength.

- Conceptual strength (inherent distinctiveness) is about the mark’s source‑indicating capacity in the abstract. Marks are classified as generic, descriptive, suggestive, arbitrary, or fanciful; a suggestive, arbitrary, or fanciful mark is “inherently distinctive” and presumptively strong in concept.

- Commercial strength (market strength) is the mark’s recognition and distinctiveness in the marketplace, based on consumer exposure, advertising, sales, and importantly for DuPont factor six, the extent to which others use similar marks on similar goods or services.

These two dimensions operate independently. A mark can be conceptually strong (e.g., ASPIRE is not generic for many financial services and can be an inherently distinctive standard character mark) but commercially weak in a particular field if many competitors use or have registered ASPIRE‑formative marks for similar services. Apex underscores this distinction: the Federal Circuit accepted the Board’s conclusion that CC Serve’s ASPIRE registration did not have an unusual degree of commercial strength but found the Board had not properly measured commercial weakness under factor six because it limited the universe of third‑party marks too narrowly.

The role of third‑party use and registrations

Third‑party evidence comes in two principal forms: evidence of actual marketplace use (web pages, advertising, product packaging, screenshots, press releases, business filings) and registrations (USPTO certificates). Both matter, but they carry different weight.

- Actual use is the most persuasive evidence. Use shows that consumers encounter the mark on the relevant goods or services and that coexistence has been realized in the marketplace. Courts favor contemporaneous, corroborated examples showing sales, marketing, channels of trade, and duration of use.

- Registrations alone are weaker evidence, because a registration by itself does not prove use or marketplace recognition. Registrations, however, can be probative when corroborated by proof of use, or where they show a pattern of attempts to claim exclusive rights in a term across related services.

When assembling factor‑six proof, therefore, prioritize demonstrable use. Screenshots with dates, advertising spend, sales figures, citations to industry directories, and evidence that marks reach the same consumer class are all helpful. The Apex opinion reflects the TTAB’s use of both registrations and use evidence, but the Federal Circuit emphasized that the scope of marks the Board considered should reflect the similarity finding made under factor two.

Crowded‑field rationale and consumer education

The crowded‑field doctrine rests on an empirical claim about consumer behavior: exposure to many similar marks teaches consumers to discriminate among them, reducing the likelihood that one more similar mark will cause confusion. Courts consider not just raw counts of similar marks but whether those marks are sufficiently visible and widespread to have educated consumers. The existence of many obscure registrations filed but never used is much less persuasive than a smaller number of prominent, widely used marks.

Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin illustrate the inquiry. Juice Generation found “a considerable number” of third‑party marks (26) and treated that evidence as a basis for narrowing; Jack Wolfskin relied on a catalog of design and word uses (about 14 examples) to show the field was crowded. But those numbers are suggestive, not controlling facts. Apex makes this point explicit: the Federal Circuit reversed the Board for treating a set of nine uses limited to “credit card services” as insufficiently numerous—because the Board had earlier concluded that banking and credit card services were highly similar, it could not artificially narrow factor six to exclude marks used on closely related financial services.

No bright‑line numbers

It is tempting to search for a numerical recipe for when factor six “tips” toward weakness. The appellate decisions caution against that approach. Courts evaluate the quality, prominence, and nature of the marks as much as quantity. A small number of major, well‑known third‑party uses may be more persuasive than a larger number of obscure registrations. Conversely, a long list of mere registrations filed for narrow or unrelated services may be entitled to little weight. Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin should therefore be read as context: they show that courts may accept mid‑teens to mid‑twenties of examples as persuasive in some contexts, but they do not convert those counts into universal thresholds.

Defining “similar goods or services” under factor 6: consistency with factor 2

A critical procedural point from Apex is that the Board must be consistent in how it defines the relevant goods or services across factors. The second DuPont factor—similarity of goods or services—is commonly the core of the confusion analysis. When the Board finds, as it did in Apex, that services are “highly similar” or even partially “legally identical,” it must use that same conception of similarity when assembling and evaluating third‑party evidence under factor six.

Apex’s error illustrates the inverse problem: the Board found Apex and CC Serve’s services highly similar (banking/financing versus credit card services) for factor two but then confined its factor‑six inquiry to marks expressly used on “credit card services” only, excluding other banking and financial services uses. The Federal Circuit held that inconsistency was legally flawed. If the services are close enough that a DuPont factor two finding supports likely confusion, then third‑party marks on related banking and financing services are plainly relevant to whether the field is crowded. Put simply, you cannot broaden the goods for purposes of establishing similarity and then narrow them for purposes of proving crowding.

Relevance of broader financial services evidence when services are highly similar or partially identical

In financial‑services contexts—credit cards, lending, account servicing, payment processing, and general banking functions—consumers often experience services through overlapping channels (banks, online platforms, card co‑brands, servicing companies). When the record shows close functional overlap between credit card services and broader banking/financing services, the Board should consider third‑party marks used anywhere in that cluster. Third‑party bank brands, fintech services, card issuer programs, and payment‑processing brands that use a common formative (like ASPIRE) are probative of consumer exposure and market crowding. The Federal Circuit’s remand instructs the Board to reevaluate factor six with that expanded lens.

Building a persuasive factor‑six record

Practical litigation and prosecution advice centers on how to assemble third‑party evidence that is both admissible and compelling.

1. Start with a properly scoped goods‑or‑services definition. Tie the scope to the likely consumers and trade channels (e.g., consumers of “banking and financial services” or “card issuance and account servicing”). If factor two is contested, develop evidence that supports your chosen scope because inconsistency will be fatal.

2. Prioritize actual use evidence. Screenshots of active web pages, archived pages (with dates), press releases announcing programs, product brochures, and screenshots of online account menus show real marketplace exposure. Dates matter—contemporaneous evidence near the time of the senior mark’s use or the applicant’s filings is particularly probative.

3. Corroborate registrations with use proof. Registrations can fill gaps but are not a substitute for use. If you cite a registration, attempt to show how the registrant uses the mark in commerce.

4. Demonstrate geographic and channel overlap. Evidence that third‑party marks operate in the same geographic markets and through the same channels of trade (online banking portals, co‑brand credit card programs, bank branch signage, national advertising) increases probative value.

5. Show continuity and prominence. Longstanding uses, substantial advertising, or large user bases are more persuasive than ephemeral or localized uses. Market share or advertising spend figures, when available, can materially strengthen a crowded‑field claim.

6. Provide context for consumer exposure. Consumer surveys, industry analyses, press coverage, and third‑party consumer reviews can show that consumers actually perceive multiple ASPIRE‑formative brands as distinct competitors.

7. Address distinctiveness of the third‑party marks. If many third‑party marks are descriptive or low in distinctiveness, the fact that they exist still informs crowding—but courts will consider whether consumers are likely to confuse those third‑party marks among themselves.

8. Be precise and organized. A single compilation exhibit with each example annotated for date, geographic reach, channels, and whether the evidence shows actual use or mere registration will help the tribunal evaluate the qualitative aspects of the field.

Implications of a weak field term like ASPIRE

When factor six shows a crowded field for a formative like ASPIRE in the financial services arena, several practical outcomes follow.

- Narrowed scope of protection. The senior user may be entitled only to protection in a narrow range of goods or services, or against marks that create the same commercial impression and operate in the identical channels of trade. Unlike a famous mark, it will not block all uses of the formative across the field.

- Greater tolerance for coexistence. Courts and the TTAB may be more inclined to permit the applicant to register or use the contested mark for distinct services or even for overlapping services if the two marks can coexist without likely confusion in practice (for example, via disclaimers, careful use of house marks, or differences in stylization and channels).

- Different remedies and doctrines. A crowded field reduces the likelihood of an injunction sweeping broadly; relief—if any—may be limited to specific goods/services or geographic channels. The crowded field also affects the weight of other DuPont factors such as fame and the senior mark’s commercial impression under factor one.

Practical checklist for factor six litigation and prosecution

- Define the relevant consumer class and channels of trade, consistent with your factor‑two theory.

- Collect contemporaneous evidence of third‑party use (screenshots with timestamps, press releases, brochures, app store pages).

- Gather registrations only as supporting evidence, and where possible, corroborate registrations with use evidence.

- Show the nature and prominence of third‑party uses: duration, advertising, market reach, and any evidence of consumer recognition.

- Organize examples by similarity of services and by commercial prominence; explain why each example is relevant to the consumer class you identified.

- Anticipate and rebut arguments that third‑party examples are geographically limited, merely descriptive, or not in the same channels.

- If you are the senior user, be prepared to show why your mark is commercially distinct despite other uses (via sales, advertising, and goodwill evidence).

Conclusion

Factor six is a fact‑intensive, context‑sensitive inquiry. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. teaches two enduring lessons. First, crowding is about market reality as much as word counts: tribunals evaluate the quality, nature, and marketplace prominence of third‑party uses, not just the number of registrations. Second, and critically for practice, the tribunal must assess similarity of goods or services consistently across DuPont factors: a finding that services are highly similar under factor two cannot be the basis for an artificially narrow universe when testing whether similar marks appear in the same marketplace under factor six. Attorneys should therefore build a factor‑six record that (1) mirrors the goods/services scope used elsewhere in the DuPont analysis, (2) emphasizes demonstrable marketplace use across those services, and (3) explains how consumer exposure to multiple similar marks has educated the relevant public. When done well, that record can deprive a senior owner of expansive rights in a common formative; when done poorly, it risks a remand or reversal like the one in Apex.

# Similarity of the Marks and Overall Commercial Impression (DuPont Factor 1)

The first DuPont factor asks whether the marks, viewed in their entireties, are similar in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression. This inquiry is the heart of a Section 2(d) analysis because a finding of similarity in the marks is the most direct route to a likelihood-of-confusion conclusion. But that inquiry is not purely mechanical. Courts and the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board must balance the holistic comparison of the marks with recognition that some components of a compound mark may be weak, descriptive, or otherwise entitled to less weight. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates both ends of that spectrum and, importantly, shows how evidence about third‑party use (DuPont factor 6) can change what dominates the consumer’s perception under factor 1.

Comparative methodology: holistic comparison, not side‑by‑side dissection
The controlling method is comparative but holistic. The marks must be considered "in their entireties" as to appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression. That principle bars courts from deciding similarity by artificially dissecting marks into segments and comparing only one segment in isolation. A holistic comparison asks whether, on balance, the ordinary consumer encountering the marks in the marketplace would be likely to believe the goods or services come from the same source.

At the same time, holistic comparison does not forbid recognizing when one element of a mark overwhelmingly dominates the consumer's perception. Courts may properly focus on the dominant portion of a composite mark—because consumers commonly shorten or emphasize the dominant element in ordinary use—but must explain why that element dominates rather than simply excising minor elements wholesale.

Dominant elements and weak components: how generic or descriptive matter is treated
Elements that are generic, highly descriptive, or widely used in a field are afforded less weight in the DuPont 1 analysis. When a mark is composed of a commonly used or generic descriptor (for example, "BANK" for banking services), that component is unlikely to function as a source identifier and will not strongly influence the overall commercial impression. In contrast, distinctive or arbitrary elements (a coined word, a fanciful device) receive greater weight.

The correct approach in practice is twofold. First, identify whether any component is generic or weak in the relevant trade. If so, explain how that weakness reduces the component's contribution to the whole. Second, assess whether the remaining elements—especially the distinctive or dominant one—create a likelihood of confusion when compared to the senior mark. The district between "dissection" and "dominance" is explanation: the adjudicator should justify why one element controls the comparison based on marketplace reality, not ipse dixit.

Standard character registrations: breadth and implications
A standard (word) character registration confers rights in the word itself, independent of any particular font, stylization, or design. That breadth is significant: a standard character mark like CC Serve’s ASPIRE protects the word in any typographic or design context the registrant may adopt (subject to other limits, like descriptiveness or weakness). Consequently, when a junior applicant uses the same word within a composite or design mark, the word element of the applicant’s mark is a natural focus of the first‑factor analysis.

But breadth of a standard character registration does not automatically mean that its word will always dominate the comparison. Commercial impression depends on how the term functions in the relevant market. If third‑party use shows that the word has lost distinctiveness or is widely used generically or descriptively, the protection afforded by the standard character registration will be correspondingly narrow. Thus, the registrant’s right is broad in form but conditioned in scope by the mark’s actual strength and distinctiveness in the marketplace.

Connotation and impression in financial branding: the aspirational case
Financial services provide an instructive domain. Words like ASPIRE have an inherently aspirational connotation: they suggest goals, upward mobility, or prospective success. That connotation does not, by itself, confer source‑identifying strength—it may in some cases be merely ornamental or descriptive. Whether ASPIRE functions as a strong source indicator depends on the extent to which the term is used by others in the same field and the degree to which consumers perceive it as associated with a single source.

Addition of a generic or descriptive term to a distinctive word can change the balance of the commercial impression. For example, ASPIRE + BANK pairs a possibly distinctive aspirational term with a generic service identifier. If ASPIRE is distinctive (rare, arbitrary, or used as a trademark), the composite ASPIRE BANK will likely be perceived as a single mark in which ASPIRE remains the dominant source cue. If, however, ASPIRE is common in the industry, consumers may focus more on the remaining distinguishing cues (for instance, the overall design or trade dress) or may view the composite as a descriptive phrase — thereby diluting the registrant’s exclusive claim.

I.AM.Symbolic and related cases: commercial impression as the touchstone
The Federal Circuit emphasizes that commercial impression controls the DuPont 1 comparison. In re I.AM.Symbolic reiterated that courts must focus on how marks are perceived by ordinary consumers in the relevant market. That approach requires looking beyond mere literal similarity to the context—trade channels, goods or services, and the typical consumer’s selection process. Related precedents (DuPont, Palm Bay, Jack Wolfskin, Juice Generation, Olde Tyme Foods) reinforce two linked propositions: (1) marks must be compared holistically, and (2) evidence about the marketplace—especially third‑party registrations and uses—matters because it shapes consumer perception and the mark’s strength.

Design elements and trade dress cues: limits of stylization as a differentiator
Design or stylization can sometimes distinguish otherwise similar words: a distinctive logo, unique type treatment, or a non‑word design element may change the overall commercial impression. But stylization will carry weight only insofar as it is prominent and likely to alter how consumers perceive the mark. Where a standard character mark exists, the registrant’s rights cover the word across stylizations; thus a junior party’s stylization must demonstrably change the mark’s impression to overcome the word similarity.

In practice, the Board and courts ask whether the design element is so dominant that it would be the primary basis of consumer recognition. Small or conventional stylizations—especially when used in conjunction with the same dominant word—rarely prevent a finding of similarity. Conversely, a striking design that draws consumer attention and is consistently used as the source identifier can be sufficient to reduce confusion risk, but that showing often requires evidence of distinctiveness or long, exclusive use.

Interdependence of factors 1 and 6: how demonstrated weakness recalibrates the comparison
Apex Bank shows the doctrinal interdependence of DuPont factors. The Board found the parties’ services highly similar under factor 2, but when it turned to factor 6 (the number and nature of similar marks), it allowed an unduly narrow view of what third‑party uses to consider. The Federal Circuit held that, having found high similarity of services, the Board should consider third‑party marks across the full scope of similar banking and financing services—not only those limited to a narrow subset such as credit‑card services. That error mattered because evidence of widespread third‑party use of ASPIRE‑formative marks across banking and finance could demonstrate that ASPIRE is conceptually weak in that field.

Why does that matter for factor 1? If factor 6 shows that a word is crowded—used by many traders for similar services—consumers are more likely to perceive the term as descriptive or as a common brand modifier. That diminishes the term’s dominance and increases the weight of any other distinguishing features in the junior mark (other words, stylization, or overall trade dress). Conversely, scant third‑party use supports a finding that the word remains distinctive and thus likely to dominate consumers’ impressions. In short, factor 6 supplies market context that constrains how much weight the finder should give to a shared word under factor 1.

Apex applied: anticipating outcomes on remand
Applied to Apex, consider two broad remand scenarios:

- Third‑party proliferation. If, on remand, the Board finds pervasive third‑party use of ASPIRE (or close variants) across banking and finance, that evidence will show ASPIRE is a relatively weak source identifier in the relevant market. The Board would then give less weight to the shared ASPIRE element when comparing CC Serve’s ASPIRE to Apex’s ASPIRE BANK marks. Because "BANK" is generic, the overall commercial impression of ASPIRE BANK might not be dominated by the ASPIRE term; consumers may perceive ASPIRE as an ordinary modifier rather than a unique source cue. That recalibration could reduce the likelihood of confusion under factor 1, particularly if Apex’s stylization or trade dress contributes additional distinguishing cues.

- Limited third‑party use. If the Board, on remand, finds only limited use of ASPIRE across the broader banking and finance category, then ASPIRE retains greater commercial strength. In that event, ASPIRE will likely be the dominant source-identifying element in both marks, and the addition of the generic “BANK” will not materially alter the overall impression. Under that factual finding, factor 1 will favor the senior registrant and support a likelihood‑of‑confusion outcome.

Practical evidence and proof
What sorts of evidence matter when calibrating factor 1 in light of factor 6? Typical and persuasive materials include: third‑party registrations and commercial use showing how common a term is within the relevant field; advertising and marketplace samples demonstrating consumer exposure; Google search results or sales listings; and consumer testimony or survey evidence about how marks are used and perceived. The particularity of evidence matters: the Board should evaluate third‑party uses across services it has already found to be similar (here, banking and financing broadly) rather than artificially constraining the analysis to a narrower subcategory.

A compact analytical checklist for factor 1 (with factor 6 interplay)
When you analyze DuPont factor 1, apply this structured approach:

1. Define the relevant consumer and trade channels (informed by factor 2 findings).
2. Compare marks in their entireties: appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression.
3. Identify any weak, generic, or descriptive components and explain why they merit reduced weight.
4. Determine whether any element is dominant in ordinary marketplace use; justify claims of dominance with evidence.
5. Consider stylization or design elements: assess whether they are prominent enough to change consumer perception.
6. Bring in factor 6 evidence: ask whether third‑party use weakens any shared element; if so, recalibrate the weight assigned to that element in the holistic comparison.
7. Conclude whether, given the properly weighted comparison, ordinary consumers are likely to be confused.

Conclusion
Factor 1 is the principle test of mark similarity, but it cannot be applied in isolation. The commercial impression that consumers draw from a mark is molded by marketplace realities—most notably, the extent to which a shared word has become common in the trade. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. illustrates that a finding of high service similarity undercuts any rationale for narrowing the universe of third‑party uses; a correct factor‑6 analysis may reveal that a shared term is weak, and that revelation must inform the factor‑1 comparison. Students should therefore always treat the marks‑as‑a‑whole inquiry as context‑sensitive: start with the whole, identify and justify any dominant or weak elements, and fold in market evidence before drawing conclusions about likelihood of confusion.

# Strategy, Procedure, and the Significance of Apex in the Doctrinal Landscape

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. (Fed. Cir. 2025) is a compact but important decision for practitioners who litigate trademark disputes at the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and on appeal. The Federal Circuit’s opinion reaffirms familiar standards—the Board’s factual findings are reviewed for substantial evidence while legal conclusions are reviewed de novo—but it also gives a clear, practical directive about coherence in DuPont analysis: where the Board finds services “highly similar” (even “partly legally identical”) under DuPont factor two, it must carry that scope through the other DuPont inquiries, especially the sixth (third‑party use) and the first (whole‑mark similarity and commercial impression). This chapter explains the appellate mechanics that govern TTAB appeals, interprets the remedial posture of mixed outcomes (affirm‑in‑part, vacate‑in‑part, remand), draws doctrinal lessons about consistency across factors, and offers concrete strategy for record building and branding decisions in the financial services context. It situates Apex among Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin, Olde Tyme Foods, and other precedents and sets out what practitioners and the Board should do on remand.

## Appellate mechanics from the TTAB: deference, remand, and crafting arguments that survive substantial‑evidence review

Apex reiterates the canonical division of labor for appellate review of TTAB decisions. The Federal Circuit reviews the Board’s legal conclusions de novo and its factual findings for substantial evidence. Substantial evidence is not a reweighing test; it asks whether the Board’s factual conclusions are supported by such relevant evidence as a reasonable mind might accept as adequate. Thus a practitioner who expects to win on appeal must build a record that will survive an “adequate to support” review.

What to emphasize in the record. On issues likely to be characterized as factual—how services actually operate in the marketplace, the scope of a registrant’s services, the number and nature of third‑party uses, consumer sophistication, and survey results—submit admissible evidence that is contemporaneous, well‑documented, and authenticated. Typical items that carry weight on appeal are declarations or testimony from knowledgeable witnesses (with foundation for personal knowledge), dated web captures (e.g., Wayback Machine) with authentication, contemporaneous advertising and marketing materials, third‑party registrations accompanied by use evidence, and consumer surveys that withstand Daubert‑type scrutiny when survey methodology is contested. Avoid relying solely on argument or counsel commentary; the Federal Circuit will not supply the missing evidentiary bases that the Board did not have.

Preserve objections and rulings for appeal. Challenge inadmissible documents or hearsay where appropriate, and make sure the Board rules on those objections. Unruled objections or failures in the administrative record (e.g., missing foundational testimony) are difficult to resurrect on appeal.

Frame legal issues precisely. Appellate courts are most receptive to questions that separate fact and law clearly—e.g., “Did the Board err as a matter of law by applying inconsistent scopes of similarity across DuPont factors?”—and to record‑linked arguments that point to specific gaps in the Board’s reasoning or missing evidence that undermines the substantial‑evidence basis for a finding.

## Remedial posture in mixed outcomes: what “affirm‑in‑part, vacate‑in‑part, remand” requires the Board to revisit

Apex is instructive about what the Board must do after a partial vacatur. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s factor‑two finding (services highly similar) but vacated and remanded the sixth and first factors because the Board’s consideration of third‑party uses and of the marks’ commercial impression was inconsistent with its own finding about services. On remand the Board must:

- Reopen (or reconsider) the sixth DuPont analysis using a scope of third‑party marks consistent with its factor‑two finding. If the Board concluded the services are highly similar or overlap in scope, it cannot constrict the universe of third‑party marks to a narrower category (for instance, only “credit card services”) without explanation. Apex signals that the Board should consider third‑party uses across the broader class of banking and financing services when the services are found similar in that broader sense.

- Reassess the first DuPont factor (the marks’ overall appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression) in light of any altered assessment of commercial strength that arises from an expanded sixth‑factor analysis. Because a mark’s commercial strength can affect the weight of similarity in appearance or connotation, the Board must evaluate the first factor with the record and the sixth‑factor conclusions in mind.

- Remain faithful to the administrative record unless it reopens the record for new evidence. The Board may accept further evidence on remand only if it does so within the governing rules and gives parties fair notice and opportunity to respond. The remand does not give the Board carte blanche to make new factual findings unsupported by the record or to change its overall analytical approach without explaining why the new approach is warranted.

- Make explicit the linkages among factors. The Board should give a clear explanation of how its findings about services inform its assessment of third‑party use and commercial strength, and how those in turn bear on the likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus.

In short, a vacatur plus remand obliges the Board to correct identified legal errors and to reconcile its analysis across factors rather than to relitigate settled facts in an amorphous way.

## Consistency across DuPont factors: the Apex directive to align similarity assessments

Apex’s most significant doctrinal point is procedural coherence: once the Board adopts a particular factual scope for similarity under DuPont factor two, that scope is generally binding for related inquiries under other factors unless the Board articulates a good reason to narrow or broaden it. Apex cites Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation for the proposition that sixth‑factor similarity need not be limited to identical goods. The logic is straightforward:

- DuPont factor two identifies the marketplace relationship between the parties’ goods or services. If the Board finds the services to be “highly similar” or partly legally identical, it has implicitly defined the relevant product/service universe and the relevant consumer class.

- DuPont factor six asks whether there are many similar marks in use on similar goods. If the Board’s factor‑two finding establishes the breadth of similarity, then restricting the sixth‑factor search to a narrower slice (e.g., only “credit card services” when factor two encompassed “banking and financing services”) produces inconsistent results and deprives the analysis of meaningful context.

- DuPont factor one (whole‑mark similarity) is partly a function of marketplace context and consumer perception. The commercial strength of a mark—shaped by third‑party use—can affect how much weight to give similarities in appearance or connotation. Thus, the Board must consider factor one with the insights gleaned from a full sixth‑factor review.

Apex therefore instructs the Board to avoid ad hoc narrowing or redefinition of relevant universes across DuPont factors. When a factual pillar (like the similarity of services) is established, the Board must use that pillar consistently unless it provides an articulated basis for exception.

## Litigation strategy: preserving objections, curating third‑party evidence, and leveraging dictionaries and industry sources

The decision illuminates how counsel should assemble and present evidence in TTAB proceedings to maximize the odds of success on the merits and on appeal.

Curating third‑party evidence. Third‑party registrations can be persuasive on two related issues: (1) to show that goods/services overlap and (2) to show that a term is weak because many others use it. But two things matter. First, registrations are strongest when accompanied by use evidence showing actual marketplace presence. Second, define carefully the universe of comparable services: if you argue a broad overlap (banking and financing generally), include third‑party marks across that spectrum; if you argue narrow, be prepared to explain why narrow scope is appropriate and supported by the record. Use dated web evidence, marketing materials, and transaction records that show consumers encounter the third‑party marks in ways similar to the parties at issue.

Authenticating materials. For web captures, provide declarant testimony that explains how the captures were obtained and that they reflect the source and date. For third‑party advertisements or card images, include affidavits from the source or other foundation testimony. A weak foundation invites the Board to discount the evidence and provides a target for appeal.

Dictionaries and industry sources. Apex shows that the Board will define services and refer to dictionary and industry meanings where the identification in an application or registration is ambiguous or the Trademark ID Manual has changed. Use contemporary industry definitions (Federal Reserve guidance, payment‑industry glossaries, or examiner‑recognized sources) to demonstrate how consumers perceive services. If you are attempting to show the services are dissimilar, show how the parties’ services are delivered through different channels (e.g., CC Serve as a back‑office servicer for co‑branded credit cards vs. a bank offering deposit accounts), have different contractual counter‑parties, or target different customers.

Surveys and consumer sophistication. Surveys are expensive but often dispositive. Where the services are technical (financial services frequently are), argue consumer sophistication if surveys or industry evidence support that consumers exercise care; but be cautious—financial services, especially internet banking or co‑branded programs, may reach less sophisticated consumers making routine transactions. Survey methodology must be robust (proper universe, unbiased stimuli, appropriate questioning) because inadequate surveys are easily discounted at the Board and on appeal.

Preserving issues. When you anticipate appellate review, preserve evidentiary objections and legal arguments (including alternative formulations) below. The Federal Circuit rarely entertains new arguments not raised before the Board.

Practical evidence checklist. On front‑end, collect: (1) contemporaneous marketing materials; (2) screenshots with dates; (3) signed declarations establishing use and channels of trade; (4) third‑party registrations plus corroborating use evidence; (5) licensing or coexistence agreements; (6) consumer survey reports with raw data and methodology; (7) expert declarations on industry practice and consumer behavior.

## Industry‑specific guidance: branding in crowded financial vocabulary and the risks of aspirational terms

Apex involved the word “Aspire,” an archetypal aspirational term used frequently across the financial sector. The financial services field is saturated with language such as “aspire,” “trust,” “secure,” and “advantage.” Apex, together with Jack Wolfskin and Juice Generation, warns that marks using common or aspirational terms in a crowded field face two risks: (1) limited scope of protection if many others use the same formative, and (2) a greater burden to show consumer recognition of a single source.

Branding takeaways for institutions:

- Favor distinctiveness. Arbitrary or coined marks (e.g., invented words, fanciful logos) are easier to protect than descriptive or weakly suggestive aspirational terms. If a bank must use a suggestive or aspirational term for marketing reasons, pair it with distinctive stylization or a robust house mark to create a stronger source identifier.

- Narrow identifications. Consider limiting the identification of services in trademark filings to the narrowest accurate scope consistent with business plans. A narrower identification reduces overlap in the Board’s product/service universe and may shrink the pool of competing third‑party uses.

- Document exclusive use and promotion. If you rely on a suggestive mark, document the investment in advertising, market penetration, unique channels of trade, and any evidence of secondary meaning.

- Consider coexistence strategies. Financial institutions commonly enter network or co‑branding relationships. Licensing, co‑existence, or consent agreements can mitigate later oppositions and provide safe harbor for brand launches.

- Watch for channel overlap. Even if you are not offering credit cards, if your bank intends to offer online banking and consumer loans, third‑party marks in ancillary payment products (e.g., card issuance platforms, account management services) can be relevant. Apex underscores that such cross‑service uses matter when services are similar.

## Choosing between appeal routes: Federal Circuit direct appeal versus district‑court action — strategic considerations

Practitioners challenging USPTO refusals or TTAB determinations often confront a choice of forum. Apex arose on direct appeal from the TTAB to the Federal Circuit (the normal route for inter partes proceedings). Two routes to contest registration decisions deserve mention:

- TTAB decision appealed to the Federal Circuit (28 U.S.C. §1295(a)(4)(B)). This is the ordinary route for inter partes matters (oppositions, cancellations). The appeal is limited to the administrative record; no district‑court discovery is available. Advantages: specialized appellate review, relatively faster resolution of legal issues, and a tight record that can favor a party that preserved strong documentary evidence. Disadvantages: no additional discovery, and the Federal Circuit gives substantial‑evidence deference to factual findings.

- Civil action in district court (15 U.S.C. §1071) or a separate infringement action. For refusals where a party is aggrieved by the PTO director’s actions, a civil action in district court may be available (commonly invoked in ex parte appeals). District courts offer broader discovery and fact development but are slower and more costly. Also, the district court’s findings are subject to ordinary appellate review, and the case could end up at the Federal Circuit on appeal. In inter partes matters already resolved by the TTAB, there is typically no parallel district‑court route to overturn the Board’s decision—appeal to the Federal Circuit is the principal path.

Strategic considerations. If you need discovery to develop facts about third‑party uses, channels of trade, or consumer perception, a district court action (where available) may be preferable. If the dispute is primarily legal or rests on documentary evidence that is already in hand, TTAB proceedings and a Federal Circuit appeal may be quicker and more efficient. Consider litigation costs, the availability of injunctive relief versus registration relief, and the particular procedural posture (opposition, cancellation, ex parte refusal).

## Implications for examiners and the Board: defining relevant public, interpreting legacy identifications, and weighing third‑party landscapes

Apex has lessons for examiners and the Board when they define the scope of services or evaluate third‑party evidence:

- Treat identification meanings carefully. The Board in Apex parsed the registrant’s “credit card services” to include a range of activities (issuing cards, account management, processing). When an identification in a registration or application is ambiguous or where the ID Manual has evolved, the Board should explain how it construes the services and why. Examiners should issue office actions that clarify the applicant’s identification and request amendment when necessary.

- Define the relevant public consistently. Whether the Board is assessing likelihood of confusion, consumer sophistication, or third‑party use, it should state who the relevant consumers are and maintain that definition across related DuPont factors unless it articulates a reasoned departure. In Apex, the Board’s narrow definition of the relevant public for the sixth factor (credit card consumers only) conflicted with its broader findings for factor two, prompting vacatur.

- Evaluate third‑party evidence holistically. The Board should consider the breadth and quality of third‑party evidence—registrations alone are weaker than registrations plus use evidence. It should assess whether the third parties are bona fide sources in the same channels of trade, whether their uses are contemporaneous and geographically relevant, and whether they target the same consumer classes.

- Be transparent about thresholds. Apex highlights how the Board used phrases like “considerable” or “ubiquitous” when rejecting the claim that nine third‑party uses demonstrated weakness. The Board should explain its quantitative and qualitative thresholds for what constitutes a crowded field, citing precedent where appropriate (e.g., Juice Generation’s “considerable number,” Jack Wolfskin’s “voluminous evidence”).

## Apex’s place in precedent: reinforcing Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin while clarifying scope‑of‑similarity across factors

Apex sits comfortably with recent Federal Circuit decisions that use DuPont factors as a flexible multi‑part inquiry rather than a mechanical checklist. Its doctrinal contributions are threefold:

1. It reinforces Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin: third‑party uses can demonstrate relative weakness of a mark and affect the scope of protection, but the Board must marshal sufficient evidence and explain why a particular number or type of third‑party marks diminishes commercial strength.

2. It invokes Olde Tyme Foods to emphasize that sixth‑factor comparisons need not be confined to identical goods or services. Apex goes further to require consistency: if factor two finds substantial similarity, factor six must consider third‑party marks across that same range of similar services.

3. It connects commercial strength (informed by factor six) to whole‑mark commercial impression under factor one, thereby emphasizing that DuPont factors are interdependent. The result is a modest but useful clarification: the Board must avoid analytical compartmentalization that treats each factor in isolation when earlier findings establish a broader factual predicate.

## Practical closing checklist: what to do before, during, and after TTAB proceedings in light of Apex

- Before filing or defending a mark: conduct broad clearance searches across related financial categories; prefer distinctive marks; if using an aspirational term, plan to document market investment and consumer recognition.

- When compiling the record: authenticate web and advertising evidence; include dated captures and declarations; gather third‑party registration and, crucially, use evidence; prepare consumer surveys if consumer perception is central.

- During TTAB proceedings: define the relevant consumer class and channels of trade early; preserve objections and alternative arguments; request or move for clarification if the Board’s interpretation of identifications is inconsistent or ambiguous.

- If appeal is likely: identify legal issues (e.g., consistency across DuPont factors) that could be argued de novo; focus on facts that can survive substantial‑evidence review; make sure the Board has an adequate evidentiary basis for its key factual determinations.

- On remand: be ready to supply or rebut additional third‑party evidence if the Board reopens the record; press the Board to explain how factor‑two findings control or influence analyses of factors one and six.

Apex may not radically change DuPont law, but it provides an important procedural check on how the Board must knit together its factor analyses. Where the Board draws a market boundary for similarity, it must use that boundary coherently across related DuPont inquiries, and practitioners should build records knowing that these interconnections will be scrutinized on appeal.